        exp: "(C) is the best response, because contract duties that are personal in nature and depend on the unique artistic skill, taste, and reputation of the obligor are non-delegable without the obligee's consent.\n\nUnder Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 318(2), an obligor cannot delegate a duty of performance if the obligee has a substantial interest in having that particular person perform or control the acts promised. Duties involving artistic expression, unique interior design, or specialized creative talent are classic non-delegable personal service obligations. The chef contracted with the well-known decorator specifically because of his unique, respected design style. Because the performance involved personal taste and artistic judgment, the chef was not required to accept performance from the decorator's son, regardless of how competent, reputable, or financially responsible the son was. Therefore, the chef is under no obligation to permit the son to perform.\n\n(A) is incorrect because personal service duties involving unique talent and reputation are non-delegable as a matter of law, even where the contract contains no express prohibition against delegation.\n\n(B) is incorrect because the doctrine of adequate assurances under UCC § 2-609 applies to delegable commercial sales contracts, not to contracts requiring unique personal artistry.\n\n(D) is incorrect because a novation requires the mutual assent of the obligee, and a unilateral delegation never effects a novation."
